## 1. Executive Overview
Patch management is the process of acquiring, testing, and applying code updates (patches) to operating systems, software applications, and firmware. Unpatched software vulnerabilities represent one of the most frequent initial access vectors exploited by ransomware operators and threat actors.

---

## 2. Breach Case Studies Caused by Unpatched Systems

### Case 1: Equifax Data Breach (2017)
- **Vulnerability:** CVE-2017-5638 (Apache Struts 2 Remote Code Execution)
- **Details:** Apache released a patch for a critical Remote Code Execution vulnerability in March 2017. Equifax failed to apply the patch to its public credit dispute portal. In July 2017, attackers exploited this unpatched flaw, accessing sensitive personal data (names, SSNs, dates of birth) of over 147 million consumers.

### Case 2: WannaCry Ransomware Outbreak (2017)
- **Vulnerability:** CVE-2017-0144 / MS17-010 (EternalBlue SMBv1 Exploit)
- **Details:** Microsoft released the MS17-010 security bulletin in March 2017 to fix a severe flaw in the Server Message Block (SMBv1) protocol. Two months later, the WannaCry worm swept across unpatched Windows machines globally, hitting the UK National Health Service (NHS) and crippling hospital IT systems.

